title: 'Kubeconfig ignores user.tokenFile: a kubeconfig authenticating from a token
  file sends an empty token'
updated: 2026-08-13T17:35:41Z
---

Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig->api resolves user credentials in the order token, exec, empty (Kubeconfig.pm). kubectl knows a third field: user.tokenFile, a path to a file whose content is the bearer token. It is what a kubeconfig points at when the token is rotated by something else -- a projected service account token, a sidecar refreshing it, the kubelet-managed file in an in-cluster style config written by hand.

We do not read it. Such a user matches neither token nor exec, falls into the "no token auth, might be using client certs only" branch and gets AuthToken->new(token => q{}), so every request goes out with an empty bearer token and the cluster answers 401. Nothing in the error names tokenFile, and the kubeconfig works with kubectl right next to it.

Two pieces: read the file into the token in api(), and add tokenFile to the %FILE_REFERENCES table in _resolve_file_references (#12), because kubectl relativizes it against the kubeconfig directory exactly like certificate-authority, client-certificate and client-key -- GetAuthInfoFileReferences in client-go lists all four. The table is already there and takes one more field name.

Open question for whoever picks this up: whether the file is read once at api() time or on every request. kubectl re-reads it, because the point of the field is that the content changes; Kubernetes::REST::AuthToken holds a fixed string, so reading once is the small fix and a refreshing credentials object is the honest one. Reading once already turns a guaranteed 401 into a working client until the token rotates.
